WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 205: The Babylonian Empire: The Fall of Babylon and the Writing on the Wall

1
00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:11.840
[Rebecca] Today we witness the dramatic end of the Head of Gold, the Babylonian Empire.

4
00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:18.960
[Rebecca] Within the fifty-foot-wide walls of the most fortified capital on earth, a drunken banquet sealed the fate of a dynasty.

5
00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:28.400
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of the royal madness, the sacred vessels of Jerusalem, and the writing on the wall.

6
00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:43.360
[George] Following his miraculous encounter with the Hebrew youths in the fiery furnace, Nebuchadnezzar experienced a terrifying dream of a colossal tree reaching to heaven, chopped down at divine command with only its stump banded with iron and bronze in the earth.

7
00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:47.120
[George] The prophet Daniel interpreted the vision with grave sorrow.

8
00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:57.280
[George] The towering tree was Nebuchadnezzar himself, whose heart would be taken from him until seven seasons passed over him, to prove that the most high rules in the kingdom of men.

9
00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:14.240
[George] Twelve months later, while walking upon the royal palace roof admiring the sprawling metropolis, Nebuchadnezzar boasted in unrestrained pride, Is not this great Babylon that I have built for the house of the kingdom by the might of my power, and for the honor of my majesty?

10
00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:19.440
[George] While the words were still in the king's mouth, a supernatural voice fell from heaven.

11
00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:25.840
[George] Instantly, Nebuchadnezzar was driven from men, struck with clinical lycanthropy or bone thropy.

12
00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:34.320
[George] He ate grass as oxen, his body was wet with the dew of heaven, his hair grew like eagle's feathers, and his nails like birds' claws.

13
00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:40.080
[George] For seven agonizing years, the master of the ancient world lived as a wild beast.

14
00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:51.920
[George] Only when he lifted his eyes to heaven and praised the eternal king did his reason return, restoring him to his throne with greater honor, confessing that those who walk in pride God is able to obey.

15
00:01:51.920 --> 00:02:01.880
[George] In royal cuneiform inscriptions unearthed by modern archaeologists, Nebuchadnezzar's builders repeatedly stamped his name upon millions of kiln-fired bricks.

16
00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:10.440
[George] Nebuchadnezzar, king of Babylon, who cares for the sanctuaries of Esagilla and Ezeda, eldest son of Nabapolisar.

17
00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:17.880
[George] Yet behind this monumental masonry lay the spiritual reality that earthly empires are fleeting before the sovereignty of God.

18
00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:24.440
[George] Two decades earlier, Nebuchadnezzar had executed the most traumatic catastrophe in Jewish history.

19
00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:35.800
[George] Frustrated by repeated Judean rebellions backed by empty Egyptian promises, the Babylonian army laid a brutal, 18-month siege to Jerusalem beginning in 588 BC.

20
00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:44.360
[George] Inside the holy city, famine reached unspeakable depths, with citizens boiling leather armor and mothers weeping over starving infants.

21
00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:50.520
[George] In the summer of 586 BC, Babylonian siege engines breached the northern wall.

22
00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:55.720
[George] King Zedekiah fled toward Jericho by night, but was captured on the plains.

23
00:02:55.720 --> 00:03:06.280
[George] Forced to watch his young sons slaughtered before his eyes at Riblah, Zedekiah had his eyes gouged out and was bound in bronze fetters, hauled in total darkness to Babylon.

24
00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:11.880
[George] Nebuzeredon, captain of the Babylonian royal bodyguard, torched Jerusalem.

25
00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:19.960
[George] The magnificent temple of Solomon, the crown jewel of Mount Moriah standing for four centuries, was thoroughly burned to the ground.

26
00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:31.000
[George] The sacred gold and silver basins, censors, bowls, and the colossal bronze sea supported by twelve oxen were smashed and hauled away to the treasury of Marduk in Babylon.

27
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:36.840
[George] The city walls were leveled to the dust, and thousands of weeping captives were marched into exile.

28
00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:47.040
[George] The siege of Jerusalem in 586 BC was not merely a military defeat, but an existential catastrophe that shattered the ancient Davidic kingdom.

29
00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:57.120
[George] The Ark of the Covenant vanished from historical records during this destruction, hidden by priests or buried beneath the rubble, never to be seen in the Second Temple era.

30
00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:05.440
[George] Transported over 800 miles across the desert sands, the Jewish exiles arrived in the alluvial plains of southern Mesopotamia.

31
00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:13.360
[George] Settled along the irrigation canals connecting the Tigris and Euphrates, they poured out their grief in immortal, haunting poetry.

32
00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:19.200
[George] By the rivers of Babylon, there we sat down, yea, we wept, when we remembered Zion.

33
00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:22.400
[George] We hanged our harps upon the willows in the midst thereof.

34
00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:31.920
[George] For there they that carried us away captive required of us a song, and they that wasted us required of us mirth, saying, Sing us one of the songs of Zion.

35
00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:35.280
[George] How shall we sing the Lord's song in a strange land?

36
00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:38.880
[George] Yet the Babylonian exile was not merely a tragedy.

37
00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:42.880
[George] It was the theological crucible that forged rabbinic Judaism.

38
00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:51.120
[George] Deprived of their temple and animal sacrifices, the exiles gathered in local assembly halls that became the world's first synagogues.

39
00:04:51.120 --> 00:05:03.520
[George] Scribes, under the guidance of the prophet Ezekiel and Ezra meticulously collected, edited, and preserved the sacred scrolls of the Torah and the prophets, codifying the scriptures that would endure through millennia.

40
00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:15.040
[George] While Babylonian paganism worshipped Marduk and Ishtar, the Hebrew exiles preserved their monotheistic identity, awaiting the promised seventy year restoration foretold by Jeremiah.

41
00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:20.720
[George] The cultural transformation during the 70-year exile fundamentally preserved Jewish heritage.

42
00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:34.520
[George] Scribes like Ezra and prophets like Ezekiel ensured that the Hebrew language, historical chronicles, and mosaic statutes were painstakingly recopied on sheepskin scrolls, insulating the community against pagan absorption.

43
00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:41.080
[George] By October 539 BC, the geopolitical landscape had drastically shifted.

44
00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:47.880
[George] Nebuchadnezzar's descendants had succumbed to palace coups, leaving the eccentric king Nabonidus on the throne.

45
00:05:47.880 --> 00:06:01.800
[George] Devoted to the moon god Sin rather than Marduk, Nabonidus spent years in religious isolation in the Arabian oasis of Tama, leaving his arrogant young son Belshazzar as co-regent inside the colossal walls of Babylon.

46
00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:10.200
[George] Outside the fortifications, the invincible armies of Cyrus the Great, king of Medo Persia, had surrounded the metropolis.

47
00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:20.600
[George] Convinced that Babylon's double ring of fifty-foot walls and twenty years of stored grain made the citadel completely impregnable, Belshazzar decided to project defiance.

48
00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:31.960
[George] On the fateful night of October 11, 539 BC, he hosted an opulent banquet for a thousand of his lords, wives and concubines in the Grand Throne Room.

49
00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:44.680
[George] Awash in wine and intoxicated by arrogance, Belshazzar ordered the royal stewards to bring forth the consecrated gold and silver vessels that Nebuchadnezzar had looted from the Holy of Holies in Solomon's temple.

50
00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:57.000
[George] Filling the sacred chalices with wine, the king and his drunken courtiers drank toasts to idols of gold, silver, bronze, iron, wood, and stone, openly mocking the god of heaven.

51
00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:07.400
[George] Inside the grand hall of Belshazzar, the revelry was heightened by the presence of foreign wines, perfumed incense, and sacred instruments looted from conquered temples.

52
00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:15.600
[George] The king's brazen decision to desecrate the Jerusalem vessels represented the ultimate act of defiant hubris against the most high God.

53
00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:21.040
[George] In the midst of their blasphemous toasts, the music and drunken laughter stopped dead.

54
00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:33.840
[George] Directly opposite the golden lampstand illuminating the royal deis, the fingers of a disembodied human hand materialized in the air, slowly tracing glowing letters into the white plaster of the palace wall.

55
00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:38.720
[George] The biblical text describes Belshazzar's visceral, paralyzing terror.

56
00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:46.800
[George] His countenance changed, his thoughts troubled him, the joints of his hips were loosed, and his knees knocked together in trembling dread.

57
00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:53.120
[George] As the phantom hand vanished, four mysterious Aramaic words remained burning upon the plaster.

58
00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:56.080
[George] Mean, mean, teckle, a parson.

59
00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:10.240
[George] Belshazzar cried out in terror for his astrologers and Chaldean soothsayers, offering purple robes, a gold chain, and third ruler's status in the empire to anyone who could decipher the text, but none could read the divine cipher.

60
00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:17.520
[George] The Queen Mother entered the banquet hall, reminding the shattered king of Daniel, in whom was the spirit of the holy gods.

61
00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:22.240
[George] Now an old man in his eighties, Daniel was escorted into the banquet hall.

62
00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:35.920
[George] Disdaining the king's gifts, Daniel rebuked Belshazzar for failing to humble his heart as Nebuchadnezzar had, reminding him that he had lifted himself up against the Lord of Heaven, drinking from his vessels while praising lifeless idols.

63
00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:42.960
[George] The Aramaic words burned with an unearthly luminescence that illuminated the trembling faces of the Babylonian elite.

64
00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:54.080
[George] Daniel's bold confrontation with Belshazzar demonstrated that true prophetic integrity bows before no earthly potentate, declaring truth to power on the eve of imperial collapse.

65
00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:58.400
[George] Daniel looked upon the trembling king and delivered the divine indictment.

66
00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:04.440
[George] This is the writing that was written, Emene, M Ene, T Kel, U Pharsin.

67
00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:11.320
[George] This is the interpretation of the thing, Eme, God hath numbered thy kingdom, and finished it.

68
00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:16.360
[George] Tkl, thou art weighed in the balances, and art found wanting.

69
00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:21.480
[George] PRES, thy kingdom is divided, and given to the Medes and Persians.

70
00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:27.160
[George] Even as Daniel spoke the sentence, the execution was already underway outside the walls.

71
00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:37.000
[George] Greek historians Herodotus and Xenophon describe the brilliant engineering ruse executed that very night by Cyrus the Great and his Persian general Ugbaru.

72
00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:46.680
[George] Recognizing that assaulting Babylon's colossal towers was impossible, Cyrus stationed divisions of Persian engineers upstream along the Euphrates River.

73
00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:54.360
[George] Working in darkness, they breached a massive canal and diverted the river's main current into an artificial marshland depression.

74
00:09:54.360 --> 00:10:00.200
[George] Within hours, the river level running through the center of Babylon dropped to the height of a man's thigh.

75
00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:12.120
[George] The Persian soldiers, wading quietly into the riverbed beneath the water gates that the intoxicated Babylonian guards had left unlocked and unguarded amidst their drunken festival, entered the city.

76
00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:18.440
[George] Cyrus's diversion of the Euphrates remains one of the most celebrated tactical feats in military history.

77
00:10:18.440 --> 00:10:29.080
[George] By lowering the river's depth to thigh level, Persian infantry bypassed Babylon's supposedly impregnable hundred foot moats and massive bronze river gates in total silence.

78
00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:39.160
[George] In the dead of night, while the palace halls still echoed with drunken revelry and the terror of Daniel's prophecy, the Persian warriors stormed through the palace courtyards.

79
00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:42.680
[George] Belshazzar's royal guards were overwhelmed in minutes.

80
00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:48.720
[George] The biblical book of Daniel records the chilling, abrupt conclusion in a single immortal sentence.

81
00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:57.360
[George] In that night was Belshazzar the king of the Chaldeans slain, and Darius the Median took the kingdom, being about threscore and two years old.

82
00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:10.960
[George] The mighty Neo-Babylonian Empire, which had conquered the Middle East, leveled Jerusalem, and stood as the golden head of Nebuchadnezzar's vision, vanished from history in a single evening without a protracted siege.

83
00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:22.560
[George] Cyrus the Great marched triumphantly through the Ishtar gate, greeted by the Babylonian citizens not as a destructive butcher, but as a liberator who restored the ancient temples and brought order.

84
00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:36.080
[George] The head of gold had fallen, the chest and arms of silver, the mighty Medo-Persian Empire, had ascended the stage of world history, paving the way for the historic return of the Jewish exiles to their ancestral homeland.

85
00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:45.200
[George] With the death of Belshazzar, the golden head of Nebuchadnezzar's prophetic image passed into history, fulfilling the timeline revealed to Daniel.

86
00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:54.480
[George] The transition from Babylonian tyranny to Persian administrative order inaugurated a transformative era of restoration for the Jewish people.

87
00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:11.200
[Rebecca] Next week, we continue our great biblical empire saga with episode 206.

88
00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:23.360
[Rebecca] The Persian Empire, Cyrus the Great, the Cyrus Cylinder, and the Return of the Exiles, the Edict to Rebuild Jerusalem, the Royal Highway to Susa, and the laying of the Second Temple Foundations.

89
00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:27.520
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

